ok   port 0 binds an ephemeral port
ok   two keep-alive requests on one socket get two responses
ok   pipelined requests are answered in order
ok   Connection: close closes the socket after one response
ok   HTTP/1.0 without keep-alive closes after one response
ok   HEAD returns headers with Content-Length and no body
ok   every response the server writes carries the security defaults
ok   POST with a Content-Length body is echoed back
ok   malformed request line gets 400 and the connection closes
ok   an oversized head gets 400 and the connection closes
ok   a body over max_body_bytes gets 413 and the connection closes
ok   a Content-Length too large for an Integer gets 413
ok   a zero-padded Content-Length is read as its value
ok   a head over max_head_bytes with no blank line yet gets 400
ok   a pipelined multibyte body is framed by bytes, not characters
ok   a body with NUL bytes is framed by bytes, not cut at the NUL
ok   chunked transfer encoding gets 411 and the connection closes
ok   an invalid Content-Length gets 400
ok   duplicate Content-Length headers get 400 and close the connection
ok   Transfer-Encoding beside a Content-Length is refused, and the bytes behind it are never read as a request
ok   Transfer-Encoding: identity is a transfer coding too: 411
ok   a rejected request ends the connection: a request pipelined behind it is not answered
ok   Expect: 100-continue gets no interim response; the body is awaited and answered
ok   a head ended by bare LFs is not a head: nothing is answered and the connection closes at read_timeout
ok   an absolute-form target is not rewritten: the handler sees it as the path
ok   HTTP/1.1 without a Host header is served: the host is HostAuthorization's to check
ok   HTTP/1.0 with Connection: keep-alive keeps the connection
ok   an exception in the app gets 500 and the connection stays usable
ok   parameters past Query's limits get 400, not 500, and the connection stays usable
ok   a JSON::NestingError in the app gets 500, the connection is closed and the server keeps serving
ok   a JSON::ParserError in the app gets 500 and the server keeps serving
ok   an idle keep-alive connection is closed after read_timeout
ok   a failed accept is logged and the server keeps accepting
ok   stop makes the port refuse connections
ok   serves a bare HttpHandler with no Context or Middleware involved
ok   run serves until stop is called
ok   request_stop (what the TERM trap calls) makes run return without joining
ok   request_stop waits for a request in flight and answers it completely
ok   an idle keep-alive connection does not hold up shutdown
ok   many idle keep-alive connections are closed by the drain, not waited out
ok   drain_timeout bounds the wait for a request that takes too long
ok   start raises Cybertrain::PortInUse when the port is already bound
ok   PortInUse names the reason the port could not be bound
ok   starting a server on port 80 as non-root reports permission denied
ok   reuse_port lets two servers bind one port and both answer
ok   reuse_port survives stop: the port binds again and the collector leaves the number alone
ok   a plain listener on the port still raises PortInUse for reuse_port
ok   a reuse_port listener on the port still raises PortInUse for a plain server
ok   reuse_port stops on a name with several addresses and binds a name with one
ok   reuse_port on an IPv6 literal binds it or says why not
ok   reuse_port on a host that does not resolve stops with a message naming the setting
ok   reuse_port on an address this machine does not have stops with the same message
ok   reuse_port is off unless asked for
ok   listen_fd serves on an inherited listener instead of binding the port
